window.ND=window.ND||{};Object.assign(ND,{"ae|Apparatus for amphibian experiments":{"d":"## Purpose\nClassical amphibian experiments record the mechanical responses of frog skeletal muscle and heart on a **moving surface**, against **time**. Knowing the apparatus is essential for both wet-lab demonstrations and for interpreting the graphs shown in **CBME simulations** (live dissection in UG teaching has been restricted in India since 2011–12).\n\n## Kymograph\n- A **drum** covered with glazed paper, smoked with a soot layer, rotated by a clockwork or electric motor at selectable speeds (**slow ≈ 1–2.5 mm/s; fast ≈ 25 mm/s or more**; exact speeds differ between models).\n- **Sherrington–Starling** type drums are electrically driven.\n- Writing points scratch the soot. Finished tracings are **fixed in varnish/shellac** (e.g. colophony in alcohol).\n- Fast speed is used for a single twitch (to resolve its phases); slow speed for fatigue, staircase and drug effects.\n- An **eccentric contact** (**spring contact**) on the drum spindle can trigger the stimulus at the same point each revolution, so successive twitches superimpose.\n\n## Stimulators\n- **Du Bois-Reymond inductorium** (induction coil): primary circuit with a battery (≈ 2 V cell) and key; secondary coil delivers the shock.\n  - **\"Make\" shock**: closing the primary.\n  - **\"Break\" shock**: opening it. **Stronger and quicker**, because the current falls more abruptly than it rises (self-induction opposes the make).\n  - Strength is set by sliding the secondary coil (distance).\n  - The **Neef's hammer** (Wagner interrupter) delivers repeated (faradic) shocks for tetanus.\n- **Electronic square-wave stimulators** (modern) allow precise **voltage, duration and frequency**.\n\n## Keys\n- **Simple (Morse/tapping) key** makes or breaks the primary circuit.\n- **Short-circuit (Du Bois-Reymond) key** in the secondary circuit. When closed, it short-circuits the electrodes, **preventing the unwanted make (or break) shock** from reaching the tissue.\n- **Pohl's commutator**: reverses current direction.\n\n## Electrodes\n**Non-polarisable electrodes** (e.g. zinc–zinc sulphate, silver–silver chloride) avoid polarisation during constant-current studies. Simple platinum or silver wire electrodes suffice for brief shocks.\n\n## Levers and holders\n- **Muscle trough / moist chamber**: keeps the preparation moist with **frog Ringer**.\n- **Simple (straight) lever**: free-loaded; the weight is hung near the fulcrum.\n- **After-loaded lever**: a screw/support stop prevents stretching of the muscle by the load at rest, so the load is felt **only after contraction starts**.\n- **Starling's heart lever** with a serrefine clip for the frog's heart.\n- **Magnification** = length of the lever from the fulcrum to the writing point ÷ distance from the fulcrum to the muscle attachment (typically **4–10×**).\n\n## Time recording\n- **Electrically maintained tuning fork** with a **Jaquet chronograph / signal marker**.\n- A **100 Hz** fork gives **0.01 s per wave**; a 50 Hz fork gives 0.02 s.\n- **Signal marker** (electromagnetic) records the instant of stimulation.\n\n## Frog Ringer (approximate)\n- NaCl ≈ **0.6–0.65%**\n- KCl ≈ 0.014%\n- CaCl₂ ≈ 0.012%\n- NaHCO₃ ≈ 0.02%\n- glucose ≈ 0.1–0.2%\n\n**Isotonic for amphibians**; mammalian saline is 0.9%.\n\n## Precautions and sources of error\n- Keep tissues moist and avoid metal contact with the nerve.\n- Lever horizontal; writing point tangential and light (minimises **friction**).\n- **Correct drum speed**; even smoking.\n- Use the short-circuit key to avoid double stimulation.\n- Fatigue from too frequent stimuli.\n- Electrode polarisation.\n- Temperature variation.","q":{"b":["Kymograph – smoked drum; fast for twitch, slow for fatigue/drug effects.","Inductorium: break shock stronger than make shock.","Neef's hammer gives repeated (faradic) stimuli.","Short-circuit key cuts off the unwanted shock.","Non-polarisable electrodes: Zn–ZnSO₄, Ag–AgCl.","After-loaded lever: load acts only once contraction starts.","Magnification = writing arm / load arm.","Starling's heart lever for frog heart.","100 Hz fork → 0.01 s per wave.","Frog Ringer NaCl ≈ 0.65%.","Fix tracings in varnish/shellac."],"n":["Time marker 100 Hz = 0.01 s","Ringer NaCl 0.6–0.65%","Lever magnification 4–10×"],"m":"**\"Break Beats Make\"** – the break shock is stronger.","v":["Why is the break shock stronger than the make shock? On make, self-induction slows the rise of current; on break, the current falls abruptly.","Function of the short-circuit key? Prevents the unwanted make or break shock reaching the tissue.","What is an after-loaded lever? One where a support takes the load at rest, so the muscle bears it only after it starts contracting."]}},"ae|Simple muscle twitch (gastrocnemius–sciatic preparation)":{"d":"## Principle\nA **single, adequate (threshold or supramaximal) stimulus** applied to the sciatic nerve (or directly to the muscle) produces **one brief contraction followed by relaxation**: a **simple muscle twitch**. Recorded on a fast-moving kymograph, it shows three phases.\n\n## Preparation\n- A **pithed frog** (brain and cord destroyed: decerebrate, no pain). Today this is done by simulation.\n- The **gastrocnemius** is dissected with the **tendo Achilles** and the **sciatic nerve** intact up to the vertebral column.\n- The **femur** is clamped in the muscle trough; the tendon is tied to the lever.\n- The nerve lies on the electrodes; everything is kept moist with **frog Ringer**.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Set the lever horizontal with a small load (≈ **5–10 g**; free-loaded).\n2. Set the drum at **fast speed**.\n3. Record the time trace (**100 Hz** tuning fork) and the signal-marker line beneath the muscle trace, with all writing points in the **same vertical line**: check by rotating the drum by hand (\"**alignment**\").\n4. Deliver a single **break shock** via the eccentric contact. Record the twitch.\n5. Drop perpendiculars from the start of stimulus, start of contraction, peak and end of relaxation. Measure each phase with the time trace.\n\n## Phases and normal values (frog, ≈ 20 °C)\n- **Latent period ≈ 0.01 s (10 ms)**: from stimulus to the beginning of contraction. Includes:\n  - nerve conduction and the **NMJ delay** (≈ 0.5–1 ms per synapse)\n  - **excitation–contraction coupling**: AP spread along the sarcolemma and T-tubules, **Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum**\n  - taking up the **series elastic** slack\n  - instrumental lag (inertia of the lever)\n  - **True latent period ≈ 2–3 ms** in isolated fibres.\n- **Contraction period ≈ 0.04 s**: cross-bridge cycling while Ca²⁺ is bound to **troponin C**.\n- **Relaxation period ≈ 0.05 s**: Ca²⁺ is pumped back by **SERCA** (needs ATP). Slower than contraction.\n- **Total ≈ 0.1 s**.\n\nSources differ slightly; for example, some texts give contraction 0.04 and relaxation 0.05, others both ≈ 0.04–0.05.\n\n## Mammalian comparison\n- Fast muscle (e.g. extraocular) twitch ≈ **7–10 ms** in duration.\n- Slow muscle (soleus) **≈ 100 ms**.\n- Gastrocnemius (mammal) ≈ 30–40 ms (Guyton).\n- **Muscle action potential lasts only ≈ 2–5 ms** and is over before contraction peaks, which allows **summation and tetanus**.\n\n## Strength of stimulus vs response (nerve–muscle preparation)\n- **Subthreshold**: no response.\n- **Threshold**: minimal contraction.\n- **Submaximal**: increasing height as more motor units are **recruited** (each fibre obeys all-or-none, the whole muscle does not).\n- **Maximal**: all fibres active.\n- **Supramaximal**: no further increase.\n\n## Isotonic vs isometric\n- Lever recording with a free load is essentially **isotonic** (length changes, tension constant).\n- With a rigid transducer it is **isometric** (tension measured).\n\n## Sources of error\n- Writing points not aligned, so the latent period is wrongly measured.\n- Lever friction or excess inertia (heavy lever prolongs the latent period).\n- Wrong drum speed: phases are compressed.\n- Drying of the preparation.\n- Fatigue from repeated stimulation.\n- Stimulating the muscle directly via current spread.\n- Temperature variations.","q":{"b":["One stimulus → one twitch.","Pithed frog; gastrocnemius with sciatic nerve.","Fast drum; 100 Hz time marker; points aligned.","Latent period ≈ 0.01 s.","Contraction ≈ 0.04 s; relaxation ≈ 0.05 s; total ≈ 0.1 s.","True latent period in fibres ≈ 2–3 ms.","LP includes conduction, NMJ delay, E-C coupling, elastic slack, lever inertia.","Relaxation slower – SERCA needs ATP.","AP (2–5 ms) ends before contraction peaks.","Graded response via recruitment; each fibre all-or-none.","Lever recording ≈ isotonic."],"n":["LP 0.01 s","Contraction 0.04 s","Relaxation 0.05 s","Total 0.1 s"],"m":"Twitch timings **\"1-4-5\"** (hundredths of a second): Latent 1, Contraction 4, Relaxation 5.","v":["Causes of the latent period? Conduction time, NMJ delay, excitation–contraction coupling, elastic slack and instrumental lag.","Why is relaxation longer than contraction? Active Ca²⁺ reuptake into the SR by SERCA takes time.","Why does a whole muscle give a graded response? Progressive recruitment of motor units, although each fibre obeys all-or-none."]}},"ae|Effect of temperature on muscle twitch":{"d":"## Principle\nMuscle contraction depends on **enzyme and chemical processes**:\n- **myosin ATPase** cross-bridge cycling\n- **Ca²⁺ release and reuptake** (SERCA)\n- ion channel kinetics\n\nAll of these are temperature-sensitive, with a **Q₁₀ ≈ 2–3** (the rate doubles or triples per 10 °C rise) within physiological limits. **Viscosity** of the sarcoplasm also falls with warmth.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Set up the gastrocnemius–sciatic preparation (simulation in CBME) in a muscle trough with a **thermometer**. The drum is at fast speed with the time marker.\n2. Record a control twitch at **room temperature (≈ 20–25 °C)**.\n3. Replace the Ringer with **cold Ringer (≈ 5–10 °C)**; wait 1–2 min; record a twitch **superimposed** at the same drum position (eccentric contact).\n4. Replace with **warm Ringer (≈ 30–35 °C)**; record.\n5. Optionally ≈ **40–45 °C**: heat rigor.\n6. Compare the latent period, contraction and relaxation times, and amplitude.\n\n## Observations\n### Warm (≈ 30–35 °C)\n- **All phases shortened**: latent period, contraction and relaxation.\n- **Height increased**: faster, more forceful cross-bridge cycling; reduced viscosity; possibly increased enzyme activity.\n\n### Cold (≈ 5–10 °C)\n- **All phases prolonged**, **relaxation especially** (SERCA slow).\n- **Height reduced**: increased viscosity, slower chemical reactions.\n\nSome texts note a slight height increase with moderate cooling, because the prolonged active state allows more tension. Most practical manuals (AK Jain, Ghai) teach **reduced height in cold**, so note this as a source variation.\n\n### Very high (> 40–42 °C in frog)\n**Heat rigor**:\n- Irreversible **coagulation of muscle proteins** (myosin) causes **permanent shortening** without an action potential.\n- The muscle becomes opaque.\n- It occurs at a lower temperature in frog muscle (≈ 40 °C) than in mammals (≈ 50 °C) (sources vary).\n\n## Human relevance\n- **Warm-up before exercise** raises muscle temperature, speeds contraction and relaxation, improves power and reduces injury.\n- **Hypothermia** slows reflexes and muscle performance (relaxation of the **ankle jerk** is slow in cold or hypothyroidism).\n- **Malignant hyperthermia** (ryanodine receptor mutation, RYR1) after halothane or succinylcholine: uncontrolled Ca²⁺ release, rigidity, hyperthermia. Treated with **dantrolene**.\n\n## Graph features\n- Three superimposed curves.\n- Warm curve: tallest and narrowest.\n- Cold curve: lowest and broadest, with a long relaxation tail.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Inadequate equilibration** time after changing the Ringer, so the muscle temperature differs from the bath.\n- Fatigue confounding the results: alternate the order and keep rest intervals.\n- Drying of the preparation.\n- Changing the load between recordings.\n- Curves not superimposed (stimulus not at the same drum point).\n- Thermometer not near the muscle.","q":{"b":["Contraction chemistry is temperature-dependent (Q₁₀ ≈ 2–3).","Warmth: all phases shorter, height greater.","Cold: all phases longer, especially relaxation; height smaller.","Some texts: slight height rise with moderate cooling.","Heat rigor > ~40 °C in frog – protein coagulation, irreversible.","Warm-up improves performance.","Malignant hyperthermia – RYR1, treat with dantrolene.","Superimpose curves using eccentric contact.","Allow equilibration after Ringer change."],"n":["Q₁₀ ≈ 2–3","Cold 5–10 °C","Warm 30–35 °C","Frog heat rigor ≈ 40 °C"],"m":"**\"Warm = Wide-awake (fast, tall); Cold = Clumsy (slow, short)\"**.","v":["Effect of warmth on a muscle twitch? All phases shorten and amplitude increases.","What is heat rigor? Irreversible contracture from coagulation of muscle proteins at high temperature.","Which phase is most prolonged by cold? Relaxation."]}},"ae|Effect of load: free-load and after-load (work done)":{"d":"## Principle\nThe load on a muscle affects:\n- its **initial length** (and hence force, via the **length–tension relationship**)\n- its **velocity and extent of shortening** (via the **force–velocity relationship**)\n\n**External work done = load × height lifted** (W = F × d). Work is **zero at zero load** and **zero at a load the muscle cannot lift** (isometric), so work is **maximal at an intermediate (optimal) load**.\n\n## Free-loaded vs after-loaded\n- **Free-load**: the weight hangs from the lever and **stretches the resting muscle**. The muscle starts contracting at a **greater initial length**.\n  - **Frank–Starling-like** effect: within limits, stretch increases the force of contraction (better actin–myosin overlap, up to the optimum sarcomere length ≈ **2.0–2.2 µm**).\n- **After-load**: an **after-loading screw** supports the lever, so the muscle is **not stretched at rest**. It meets the load **only after it starts contracting**. Initial length is constant (the effect of load alone is seen).\n\n## Procedure\n1. Gastrocnemius–sciatic preparation (or simulation); drum stationary.\n2. Record twitches with **increasing loads** (e.g. **10, 20, 30, 40, 50 … g**), moving the drum a little by hand between each so the contractions appear as **vertical lines side by side**.\n3. Repeat with the after-load screw adjusted for the same series.\n4. Measure the height of each contraction; correct for lever magnification to get the actual shortening.\n5. Calculate **work = load (g) × actual height (mm)** → g·mm (convert to joules if needed).\n6. Plot **work vs load** for both conditions.\n\n## Observations\n- **Height of contraction decreases** progressively as load increases.\n- **Work** rises, reaches a **maximum at an optimal load**, then falls to zero.\n- **Free-loaded muscle does more work** and lifts heavier maximal loads than after-loaded muscle, **because the initial stretch increases the force of contraction** (length–tension).\n- With heavy free loads the muscle is over-stretched, and the curve falls.\n\n## Physiological correlates\n- **Length–tension relationship**:\n  - active tension is maximal at the length where actin–myosin overlap is optimal (near resting body length)\n  - falls at shorter lengths (filament overlap, crowding)\n  - falls at longer lengths (fewer cross-bridges)\n- **Force–velocity (Hill) relationship**: the velocity of shortening falls hyperbolically as load increases. **Maximum power** occurs at **≈ 30% of maximal force**.\n- **Heart**:\n  - **preload** = free-load (end-diastolic volume; Frank–Starling)\n  - **afterload** = aortic pressure\n\n  This experiment models both concepts.\n- **Efficiency** of skeletal muscle: ≈ **20–25%** (the rest becomes heat).\n\n## Sources of error\n- Writing point and lever **magnification not corrected**.\n- **Fatigue**: increasing loads also fatigue the muscle, so allow rest and randomise the order.\n- After-load screw incorrectly adjusted (still stretching).\n- Lever friction; load not hung at a constant distance from the fulcrum.\n- Drying of the preparation.\n- Stimulus not supramaximal (variable recruitment).","q":{"b":["Work = load × height (actual).","Work zero at zero load and at maximal load; peak at optimal load.","Free-load stretches muscle at rest (preload).","After-load acts only after contraction starts.","Free-loaded muscle does more work (length–tension).","Height falls as load increases.","Optimal sarcomere length ≈ 2.0–2.2 µm.","Force–velocity: max power ≈ 30% of max force.","Heart analogy: preload (EDV) vs afterload (aortic pressure).","Correct height for lever magnification.","Muscle efficiency ≈ 20–25%."],"n":["Optimal sarcomere 2.0–2.2 µm","Efficiency 20–25%","Max power ≈ 30% Fmax"],"m":"**\"Free is More\"** – free-loaded muscle does more work because of initial stretch.","v":["Why does free-loaded muscle do more work? The initial stretch increases the force of contraction (length–tension relationship).","Work done at maximal (isometric) load? Zero, because there is no shortening.","Cardiac counterparts of free-load and after-load? Preload and afterload."]}},"ae|Effect of two successive stimuli (summation)":{"d":"## Principle\nSkeletal muscle has:\n- a **very short absolute refractory period (≈ 1–3 ms)**\n- an action potential that is **over long before the twitch reaches its peak**\n\nSo a **second stimulus** delivered after the refractory period but **before the muscle has fully relaxed** produces a second contraction that **adds to the first**. This is **wave (temporal) summation**, also called **superposition**.\n\n**Multiple-fibre (spatial) summation** means recruitment of more motor units, which is a different thing.\n\n## Mechanism\nThe extra contraction is greater because:\n- **More Ca²⁺** is released from the SR while some Ca²⁺ is still bound, prolonging and **raising the active state**. Troponin saturation increases.\n- The **series elastic component** has already been stretched by the first contraction, so more of the contractile force appears as external tension.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Gastrocnemius–sciatic preparation (or simulation); drum at fast speed; time marker.\n2. Two **break shocks of equal, supramaximal strength**, delivered via two contacts/keys with an **adjustable interval** (or a pair stimulator).\n3. Record:\n   - **Interval < refractory period (≈ < 2–3 ms)**: only **one twitch**. The second stimulus falls in the absolute refractory period of the muscle (or nerve).\n   - **Interval during the contraction phase**: second contraction **fused** on the first. **Height greater** than a single twitch (**maximal summation** when the second stimulus arrives early in contraction, just after the refractory period).\n   - **Interval during relaxation**: a **second peak** rising from the relaxation limb, still higher than a single twitch (**incomplete summation**).\n   - **Interval after complete relaxation (> 0.1 s)**: **two separate, equal twitches** (possibly a slight **staircase / beneficial effect**).\n\n## Observations and interpretation\n- The summed height can be up to **≈ 1.5–2 times** a single twitch (sources differ).\n- Shows that skeletal muscle **cannot be refractory during contraction**: the basis of **tetanus** with repetitive stimulation.\n- **Cardiac muscle**, with a **long refractory period** (≈ 250 ms, lasting through systole), **cannot summate or be tetanised**. An important functional contrast, for pumping.\n\n## Relevance\n- In the body, **motor neurons fire repetitively** (≈ 8–50 Hz, up to 100 Hz briefly). Graded force is produced by:\n  - **rate coding** (temporal summation)\n  - **recruitment** (size principle: small units first, Henneman)\n- Twitch force is only a fraction (about 20–30%) of tetanic force, so normal movements use summated contractions.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Unequal stimulus strengths (submaximal, so recruitment differs).\n- Wrong timing of contacts or drum speed.\n- Fatigue; drying.\n- Lever inertia causing artefactual \"fusion\".\n- Electrode polarisation reducing the second stimulus.","q":{"b":["Skeletal muscle ARP ≈ 1–3 ms – short.","AP ends long before twitch peaks.","Second stimulus during contraction → summation (superposition).","Interval < ARP → single twitch only.","Early in contraction → maximal summation.","During relaxation → incomplete summation (two peaks).","After complete relaxation → separate twitches.","Mechanism: more Ca²⁺ / active state + series elastic stretched.","Basis of tetanus.","Heart cannot summate – long refractory period.","Force graded by rate coding + recruitment."],"n":["Skeletal ARP 1–3 ms","Cardiac RP ≈ 250 ms","Summed height ≈ 1.5–2× twitch"],"m":"**\"Second Stimulus Stacks\"** if it comes before relaxation ends.","v":["Why does skeletal muscle summate? Its refractory period is short and ends before contraction has finished.","Result if the second stimulus falls within the refractory period? Only a single twitch.","Why can't cardiac muscle summate? Its long refractory period lasts through systole."]}},"ae|Genesis of tetanus":{"d":"## Principle\nWhen **repetitive stimuli** reach a skeletal muscle at a frequency where each new stimulus arrives **before the previous contraction has relaxed**, successive contractions **summate**.\n\n- **Incomplete (unfused, clonic) tetanus**: at moderate frequencies the record shows a **wavy** plateau (partial relaxation between stimuli).\n- **Complete (fused) tetanus**: at higher frequencies the record is a **smooth sustained plateau**.\n\nThe **fusion frequency** depends on twitch duration:\n- **Frog gastrocnemius ≈ 25–35 Hz** (sources give ≈ 20–40 Hz)\n- **Mammalian fast muscles** fuse at higher rates (≈ 60–100 Hz or more)\n- **Slow muscles** fuse at lower rates (≈ 20–30 Hz)\n- Rough rule: fusion occurs when the stimulus interval is less than about **one-third to one-half of the contraction time**.\n\n## Why tetanic tension exceeds twitch tension\n**Tetanic tension ≈ 3–5 times twitch tension** (Guyton: up to ≈ 4×). Reasons:\n1. A single twitch is too brief for the **active state** to fully stretch the **series elastic elements**. With repeated stimuli these are fully stretched, so the whole contractile force is transmitted.\n2. **Sustained high cytosolic Ca²⁺** keeps troponin **saturated**, allowing maximal cross-bridge cycling.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Gastrocnemius–sciatic preparation (or simulation); drum at **slow-moderate speed**.\n2. Stimulate with an **electronic stimulator** (or inductorium with Neef's hammer) at increasing frequencies, e.g. **1, 2, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 40 Hz**, each for **≈ 1–2 s**, with **rest intervals** to avoid fatigue.\n3. Record:\n   - **1–2 Hz**: separate twitches; may show **staircase (Treppe)**, a slight progressive rise in height.\n   - **≈ 5–15 Hz**: **clonus / incomplete tetanus**, a saw-tooth top.\n   - **≈ 20–40 Hz**: **complete tetanus**, a smooth plateau.\n4. If stimulation continues, the plateau declines (**fatigue**).\n\n## Physiological significance\n- **All voluntary contractions are brief tetani**: motor-unit firing at ≈ 8–50 Hz, **asynchronous** between units, giving smooth force.\n- **Rate coding and recruitment** grade force.\n- **Pathological tetanus**:\n  - **tetanus toxin** (*Clostridium tetani*) blocks **inhibitory interneurons** (glycine, GABA release). Causes **sustained spasm**, trismus, risus sardonicus. India achieved **maternal and neonatal tetanus elimination in 2015**.\n  - **hypocalcaemic tetany** (Trousseau's and Chvostek's signs): nerve hyperexcitability.\n\n  These are different from experimental tetanus.\n- **Heart cannot be tetanised**: its **absolute refractory period (≈ 250 ms)** covers almost the whole contraction. Essential for **rhythmic filling**.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Fatigue** from long stimulation trains: keep trains short and give rest.\n- Submaximal stimulus: recruitment varies.\n- Drying of the preparation.\n- **Lever inertia**: a heavy or oscillating lever falsely smooths a \"complete\" tetanus.\n- Wrong frequency calibration.\n- Electrode polarisation during long trains.\n- Stimulus spread directly to the muscle.","q":{"b":["Repetitive stimuli before relaxation → summation.","Incomplete tetanus: wavy plateau.","Complete tetanus: smooth plateau.","Frog gastrocnemius fusion ≈ 25–35 Hz (20–40).","Fast muscles fuse at higher frequency.","Tetanic tension ≈ 3–5 × twitch.","Reasons: series elastic fully stretched, Ca²⁺ sustained.","Low frequency (1–2 Hz) shows staircase (Treppe).","Voluntary contractions are asynchronous tetani.","Heart cannot be tetanised – long ARP (~250 ms).","Tetanus toxin blocks inhibitory interneurons (glycine/GABA).","Fatigue appears with sustained stimulation."],"n":["Fusion ≈ 25–35 Hz (frog)","Tetanic ≈ 3–5× twitch","Motor units fire 8–50 Hz","Cardiac ARP ≈ 250 ms"],"m":"**\"Wavy then Smooth\"** – incomplete then complete tetanus as frequency rises.","v":["Difference between incomplete and complete tetanus? Partial relaxation between stimuli (wavy) vs no relaxation (smooth plateau).","Why is tetanic tension greater than twitch tension? Series elastic elements are fully stretched and Ca²⁺ stays high.","Why can't the heart be tetanised? Its absolute refractory period lasts nearly the whole contraction."]}},"ae|Fatigue in skeletal muscle":{"d":"## Principle\n**Fatigue** is the **progressive decline in the ability of muscle to generate force or power** during repeated or sustained activity. It is **reversible by rest**.\n\nIn the frog **nerve–muscle preparation**, repeated stimulation through the nerve shows that fatigue occurs **first at the neuromuscular junction (NMJ)**:\n- depletion of releasable **acetylcholine** stores\n- **the nerve is practically indefatigable** (it needs little energy and the Na⁺/K⁺ pump keeps up)\n- **muscle fatigues later**\n\n## Procedure\n1. Gastrocnemius–sciatic preparation (or simulation); **slow drum**; moderate load.\n2. Stimulate the **nerve** with supramaximal shocks at **≈ 1/s** (or a contact per drum revolution) until contractions become very small.\n3. Now stimulate the **muscle directly**: it **contracts again** (vigorously). So the **muscle itself is not yet fatigued, and the block is at the NMJ**.\n4. Rest for ≈ 5–10 min; then stimulate the nerve again: **recovery** is seen.\n5. Optionally: **Bernstein's / two-electrode experiment** with curare or cold block, showing the **nerve still conducts** after prolonged stimulation.\n\n## Changes in the fatigue curve\n- **Progressive fall in height** (first after a possible initial rise: **Treppe**).\n- **Latent period lengthens**.\n- **Contraction and especially relaxation are prolonged** (slow SERCA Ca²⁺ reuptake; reduced ATP).\n- **Contracture / incomplete relaxation** at the end: the baseline does not return. Due to **ATP depletion** (rigor-like cross-bridges).\n\n## Mechanisms in intact human muscle\n- **Central fatigue**: reduced motor drive from the CNS (motivation, serotonergic mechanisms).\n- **Peripheral fatigue**:\n  - **Pi accumulation** (from phosphocreatine breakdown): main factor in high-intensity work. Reduces cross-bridge force and Ca²⁺ release.\n  - **H⁺ (acidosis)**: a role, though lactate itself is no longer considered the main cause (current view).\n  - **Glycogen depletion** in endurance exercise (\"hitting the wall\").\n  - Extracellular **K⁺ accumulation**, reducing excitability.\n  - Impaired **excitation–contraction coupling** (Ca²⁺ release).\n- **Transmission fatigue** at the NMJ occurs mainly in disease: **myasthenia gravis** causes a decremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation (> 10% decrement).\n- Recovery: **PCr resynthesis half-time ≈ 30 s**, needing oxidative metabolism. Massage and circulation remove metabolites.\n\n## Ischaemia\n- Occluding blood supply (BP cuff in human **Mosso's ergography**) accelerates fatigue markedly.\n- **Type I (slow oxidative)** fibres resist fatigue; **type IIx (fast glycolytic)** fibres fatigue rapidly.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Drying of the preparation, or a **dying preparation** mimicking fatigue.\n- Inconsistent (submaximal) stimulus strength.\n- Direct muscle stimulation by current spread during the \"nerve\" phase.\n- Load too heavy.\n- Temperature changes.\n- Electrode polarisation reducing the effective stimulus.","q":{"b":["Fatigue = reversible decline in force with activity.","Nerve–muscle prep: NMJ fatigues first.","Nerve practically indefatigable.","Direct muscle stimulation after nerve fatigue → contraction returns.","Fatigue curve: ↓ height, ↑ latent period, ↑ relaxation, contracture.","Contracture from ATP depletion.","Human: central + peripheral fatigue.","Pi accumulation key in intense exercise; lactate not main cause.","Glycogen depletion in endurance.","Ischaemia hastens fatigue.","Type I fibres fatigue-resistant; IIx fatigue fast.","Myasthenia: decrement > 10% on repetitive stimulation."],"n":["Stimulus ≈ 1/s","PCr recovery t½ ≈ 30 s","MG decrement > 10%"],"m":"Fatigue order **\"Nerve Never, Junction First, Muscle Next\"**.","v":["First site of fatigue in a nerve–muscle preparation? The neuromuscular junction.","How do you prove it? After the nerve response fails, direct stimulation of the muscle still produces contraction.","Why does contracture occur in fatigue? ATP depletion prevents cross-bridge detachment and Ca²⁺ reuptake."]}},"ae|Velocity of nerve impulse (frog sciatic nerve)":{"d":"## Principle\nThe **conduction velocity** of a nerve is found by stimulating it at **two points a known distance apart** and measuring the **difference in latent period** of the muscle's response.\n\n- The muscle, NMJ and E-C coupling delays are the same for both, so they **cancel out**.\n- **Velocity = distance between the two stimulating points ÷ difference in latent periods.**\n\nThis method was first used by **Helmholtz (1850)**.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Prepare a **gastrocnemius–sciatic preparation with a long nerve**, dissected up to the vertebral column (or a simulation).\n2. Place two pairs of electrodes: **near the muscle (point A)** and **far from the muscle (point B, near the spinal end)**. Measure the distance **AB in mm** with thread and scale.\n3. Drum at **fastest speed**, with a **100 Hz (or 500 Hz) time marker**. Use the eccentric contact so both twitches start from the **same drum position**.\n4. Stimulate at A; record the twitch.\n5. Without moving the drum start position, stimulate at B; record the second twitch **superimposed**. It starts **slightly later**.\n6. Measure the **horizontal separation** of the two curve onsets and convert it to time with the time trace.\n7. **Calculate**: e.g. if AB = 30 mm and the delay = 1 ms, velocity = 0.03 m / 0.001 s = **30 m/s**.\n\n## Normal values\n- **Frog sciatic nerve ≈ 30 m/s at room temperature**. Practical manuals quote ranges of **≈ 7–40 m/s** depending on temperature and fibre type.\n- **Human nerves (Erlanger–Gasser classification)**:\n  - **Aα: 70–120 m/s**\n  - **Aβ: 30–70 m/s**\n  - **Aγ: 15–30**\n  - **Aδ: 12–30**\n  - **B: 3–15**\n  - **C: 0.5–2 m/s**\n- **Clinical nerve conduction studies**:\n  - median motor NCV **≈ 50–60 m/s** (> **49–50 m/s normal**)\n  - ulnar ≈ 50–60\n  - common peroneal / tibial ≈ **40–50 m/s** (lower limb slower)\n  - **distal motor latency** of the median nerve ≈ < 4.2–4.4 ms\n\n## Factors affecting conduction velocity\n- **Diameter**: velocity ∝ diameter in myelinated fibres (≈ **6 m/s per µm** of total diameter, Hursh factor). Unmyelinated fibres: ∝ √diameter.\n- **Myelination**: **saltatory conduction** at the nodes of Ranvier (myelin raises speed up to about 50-fold and saves energy).\n- **Temperature**: velocity falls ≈ **2 m/s per °C** of cooling (≈ 5% per °C) in human nerves.\n- **Age**: myelination is incomplete at birth (≈ half the adult velocity), reaching adult values by **3–5 years**; it declines in old age.\n- **Disease**:\n  - **demyelination** (GBS, CIDP): **marked slowing**, conduction block\n  - **axonal neuropathy** (diabetes, alcohol): **reduced amplitude** with mildly reduced velocity\n  - **leprosy** (common in India): thickened nerves, slowed conduction\n  - **compression** (carpal tunnel): prolonged distal latency\n- Pressure affects A fibres first; local anaesthetics block C fibres first; **hypoxia blocks B fibres** first.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Inaccurate distance measurement** (the nerve is not straight).\n- Insufficient drum speed: delay too small to read (≈ 1–2 ms). Use a fast drum or an oscilloscope.\n- **Curves not superimposed** from the same start.\n- Unequal stimulus strength at A and B (different recruitment).\n- **Current spread** from proximal electrodes.\n- Temperature fluctuations.\n- Drying of the nerve.\n- Damage during dissection.","q":{"b":["Velocity = distance between stimulating points ÷ latency difference.","Muscle/NMJ delays cancel out.","Helmholtz 1850 first measured NCV.","Frog sciatic ≈ 30 m/s (7–40).","Aα 70–120 m/s; C 0.5–2 m/s.","Median nerve NCV ≈ 50–60 m/s.","Velocity ∝ diameter (myelinated ≈ 6 × D µm).","Saltatory conduction at nodes of Ranvier.","Cooling slows ≈ 2 m/s per °C.","Demyelination: marked slowing; axonal: low amplitude.","Fast drum + superimposed curves needed.","LA block C first; pressure A first; hypoxia B first."],"n":["Frog ≈ 30 m/s","Aα 70–120 m/s","C 0.5–2 m/s","Median ≈ 50–60 m/s","Hursh ≈ 6 m/s/µm"],"m":"Susceptibility **\"Pressure–A, Hypoxia–B, Local–C\"** (PAHBLC).","v":["Formula for nerve conduction velocity? Distance between two stimulating points divided by the difference in latencies.","Why does myelin increase conduction velocity? Saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier.","Which fibre type is most sensitive to local anaesthetics? C fibres."]}},"ae|Normal cardiogram of the frog heart":{"d":"## Principle\nThe **frog heart** has:\n- **two atria**\n- **one ventricle**, with a spongy, trabeculated wall and no coronary circulation (it is nourished by the blood in its lumen)\n- a **sinus venosus**: the **pacemaker**, beating at the highest rate\n- a **truncus arteriosus**\n\nIt continues beating **in situ or isolated** at room temperature in Ringer, because of its **myogenic automaticity**. Its mechanical record (**cardiogram**) shows the sequence and timing of contraction.\n\n## Preparation (traditional; now simulated)\n1. **Pith** the frog (destroy brain and spinal cord); lay it **supine** on a frog board.\n2. Open the chest (cut the sternum); remove the pericardium.\n3. A **serrefine clip** attached by thread to the **apex of the ventricle** connects to **Starling's heart lever**, balanced horizontally.\n4. Keep the heart moist with Ringer. Drum at **slow-moderate speed**; time marker in **seconds** (e.g. 1 s or 5 s signal).\n\n## Normal cardiogram\n- Each cycle shows:\n  - a **small step/notch** on the upstroke: **atrial contraction**\n  - followed by a **taller upstroke**: **ventricular systole**\n  - then a **downstroke**: **ventricular diastole (relaxation)**\n- The upstroke can be referenced as up or down depending on the lever set-up. By convention **systole is upward**.\n- Sinus venosus contraction precedes the atrial one but is too small to record with an apex clip (recorded with a separate lever via the **Stannius preparation**).\n- **Rate ≈ 30–50 beats/min** at room temperature (some manuals say 40–60). **Temperature-dependent**.\n- **Duration of one cycle ≈ 1.2–2 s**:\n  - atrial systole is short\n  - ventricular systole lasts ≈ 0.5–0.6 s\n  - diastole is longer\n\n## Key properties demonstrated with this set-up\n- **Automaticity / rhythmicity**: sinus venosus pacemaker (mammals: SA node).\n- **Conductivity**: sinus → atria → ventricle; delay at the **atrioventricular junction** (the AV ring is equivalent to the AV node).\n- **Contractility** and **all-or-none law** (functional syncytium).\n- **Staircase (Treppe)**.\n- **Long refractory period**: cannot be tetanised; extrasystole and **compensatory pause**.\n- **Starling's law**: stretch (filling) increases contraction force.\n\n## Comparison: frog vs human\n- **Frog**: 3 chambers; sinus venosus pacemaker; no coronaries in the ventricle (nourished from the lumen); **vagal + sympathetic supply in a common vagosympathetic trunk**.\n- **Human**: 4 chambers; **SA node** at **60–100/min**; coronary circulation; separate vagal and sympathetic nerves.\n\n## Precautions and sources of error\n- **Heart drying**: irrigate with Ringer frequently.\n- **Clip damaging the ventricle** or slipping.\n- Too heavy a lever or tension (over-stretch).\n- Kinked thread.\n- **Pericardium not removed**.\n- **Bleeding**: avoid cutting the abdominal vein.\n- Temperature changes alter the rate.\n- Wrong time-marker interpretation.\n- Lever friction against the drum flattening the curve.","q":{"b":["Frog heart: 2 atria, 1 ventricle, sinus venosus, truncus.","Pacemaker = sinus venosus.","Ventricle has no coronaries – nourished from lumen.","Myogenic automaticity; survives in Ringer.","Starling's heart lever with serrefine clip at apex.","Cardiogram: small atrial step then ventricular systole, then diastole.","Rate ≈ 30–50/min at room temperature.","Cycle ≈ 1.2–2 s.","Properties: rhythmicity, conductivity, contractility, excitability.","Vagosympathetic trunk in frog.","Keep moist; avoid clip damage."],"n":["Rate 30–50/min","Cycle 1.2–2 s","Human SA 60–100/min"],"m":"Frog heart parts **\"Sinus Sets, Atria Assist, Ventricle Vaults\"**.","v":["Pacemaker of the frog heart? Sinus venosus.","What is the small step on the upstroke of the frog cardiogram? Atrial contraction.","How is the frog ventricle nourished? Directly from the blood in its lumen (spongy myocardium, no coronaries)."]}},"ae|All-or-none law and staircase phenomenon (frog heart)":{"d":"## All-or-none law: principle\n**Bowditch (1871)** showed that the heart responds to a stimulus either **maximally or not at all**:\n- a **subthreshold** stimulus gives no contraction\n- a threshold or any stronger stimulus gives a **contraction of the same, maximal height** (under constant conditions)\n\nThe reason is that cardiac muscle is a **functional syncytium**. Cells are linked by **gap junctions** in the **intercalated discs**, so an action potential in one cell spreads to all (atria and ventricles each act as one unit).\n\n**Contrast with skeletal muscle**:\n- each fibre obeys all-or-none\n- the whole muscle shows **graded** responses through recruitment of motor units\n\n## Procedure\n1. Frog heart preparation (or simulation). Stop the spontaneous beats so external stimuli can be applied, by:\n   - a **first Stannius ligature** (ventricle quiescent)\n   - or using the ventricle **apex** cut off (apex is non-automatic)\n2. Stimulate the ventricle with **single induction shocks of increasing strength** (moving the secondary coil closer), at intervals longer than the refractory period (e.g. **every 10–15 s**).\n3. Record:\n   - **subthreshold**: no response\n   - **threshold**: full contraction\n   - **stronger shocks**: contractions of **equal height**\n\n## Limitations\nThe law applies only under **constant conditions**. Height varies with:\n- **initial fibre length** (Starling)\n- **temperature**\n- **ions** (Ca²⁺)\n- **drugs** (adrenaline)\n- **fatigue**\n- **interval between beats** (staircase)\n\nSo \"all\" means maximal **for the existing condition**. The law also holds for single nerve fibres and single skeletal muscle fibres.\n\n## Staircase phenomenon (Treppe, Bowditch effect)\n- After a period of rest, stimulating a quiescent ventricle with **successive identical stimuli** at short regular intervals (e.g. every 2–5 s) gives contractions that **progressively increase in height for the first few beats**, then reach a plateau.\n- This is a **positive force–frequency (rate) relationship**.\n\n### Mechanism\n- **Increased intracellular Ca²⁺**: with each beat, more Ca²⁺ enters (L-type channels) than can be extruded between beats (Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger), so the **SR Ca²⁺ load** rises, causing greater release with each beat.\n- Older explanations also cite **increased temperature, reduced viscosity and metabolites** from previous contractions (\"beneficial effect\").\n- Also seen in **skeletal muscle** at low stimulation frequencies (the mechanism there includes **myosin light-chain phosphorylation**).\n\n### Clinical relevance\n- **Force–frequency relationship** in the human heart: a modest rise in heart rate increases contractility.\n- In **heart failure** the relationship is blunted or reversed (**negative staircase**).\n\n## Sources of error\n- Spontaneous beats interfering (the ligature is not tight).\n- **Stimulus intervals too short**, falling in the **refractory period** (missed beats) or causing staircase during the all-or-none experiment.\n- Fatigue or drying of the heart.\n- Temperature change.\n- **Clip slipping**.\n- Polarisation of electrodes; current spread.","q":{"b":["All-or-none: subthreshold none; threshold+ maximal (Bowditch 1871).","Cardiac muscle = functional syncytium via gap junctions.","Skeletal whole muscle is graded (recruitment).","Law holds only under constant conditions.","Quiescent ventricle via Stannius I or isolated apex.","Staircase: successive equal stimuli → rising contractions.","Staircase mechanism: rising intracellular/SR Ca²⁺.","Positive force–frequency relationship (Bowditch effect).","Blunted or negative in heart failure.","Skeletal Treppe: myosin light-chain phosphorylation.","Stimulate at intervals > refractory period."],"n":["Stimulus interval 10–15 s (all-or-none)","2–5 s (staircase)"],"m":"**\"All or Nothing, but Steps if Rushed\"** – all-or-none at rest; staircase with repeated beats.","v":["Why does cardiac muscle obey the all-or-none law? It is a functional syncytium linked by gap junctions.","Mechanism of the staircase phenomenon? Progressive rise in intracellular (SR) calcium.","Does the whole skeletal muscle obey the all-or-none law? No, only each fibre; the whole muscle is graded by recruitment."]}},"ae|Refractory period, extrasystole and compensatory pause":{"d":"## Principle\nCardiac muscle has a **long refractory period**, because the **plateau** of the ventricular action potential is maintained by **L-type Ca²⁺ current**.\n\n- **Absolute refractory period (ARP)**: no stimulus, however strong, can excite. It lasts throughout **systole** (in mammals ≈ **0.25–0.30 s** in ventricles).\n- **Relative refractory period (RRP)**: a stronger-than-normal stimulus is needed. It lasts into **early diastole** (≈ 0.05 s).\n- **Supernormal period**: a brief period of increased excitability at the end of repolarisation.\n\nBecause the ARP lasts nearly the whole contraction, the heart **cannot be tetanised**, which ensures **rhythmic relaxation and filling**.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Frog heart on Starling's lever (or simulation); **moderate-speed drum**; signal marker.\n2. Apply **single strong induction shocks** to the **ventricle** at different phases of the cycle:\n   - **During systole** (upstroke and peak): **no response**. This shows the **ARP**.\n   - **During early diastole** (relaxation): a **premature extra contraction**, the **extrasystole**, appears (the RRP needs a stronger stimulus).\n   - **During late diastole**: an extrasystole is readily produced.\n3. Note what follows the extrasystole.\n\n## Extrasystole and compensatory pause\n- The extrasystole is **premature** and usually **smaller** (less filling, incomplete recovery). With very late stimuli it may be normal in height.\n- It is followed by a **compensatory pause**: a longer-than-normal diastole.\n- **Mechanism**:\n  - the **next natural impulse** from the sinus venosus (pacemaker) **arrives while the ventricle is still refractory** from the extrasystole, so it is **missed**\n  - the ventricle waits for the **following** sinus impulse\n- Hence the interval from the beat before to the beat after the extrasystole ≈ **two normal cycle lengths** (a \"**fully compensatory**\" pause).\n- The **post-extrasystolic beat** is **stronger**:\n  - longer filling (**Frank–Starling**)\n  - **post-extrasystolic potentiation**: extra Ca²⁺ in the SR\n- **No compensatory pause** if the stimulus is applied to the **sinus venosus**: it resets the pacemaker.\n- Mammals:\n  - **ventricular premature beats (VPCs)** show a **full compensatory pause** (the SA node is not reset)\n  - **atrial premature beats** usually show an **incomplete (non-compensatory) pause** (the SA node is reset)\n\n## Clinical relevance\n- **Ectopic beats (extrasystoles)**: palpitations, a \"**missed beat**\" sensation (actually the pause and the strong following beat).\n- **Pulse deficit** with early ectopics (insufficient filling).\n- The **R-on-T phenomenon** (stimulus in the vulnerable period near the T-wave peak) can trigger **ventricular fibrillation**.\n- Antiarrhythmics (**class III**: amiodarone) **prolong the refractory period**.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Stimulus too weak** (fails in the RRP, mistaken for ARP).\n- Stimulating the **atria or sinus**: different result, no compensatory pause.\n- Electrode slipping.\n- Spontaneous rate irregular.\n- Signal marker misaligned with the curve.\n- Drying of the heart; temperature change.","q":{"b":["Cardiac ARP covers systole (~0.25–0.30 s, mammal).","Long ARP due to Ca²⁺ plateau → no tetanus.","Stimulus during systole: no response.","Stimulus in diastole: extrasystole.","Extrasystole followed by compensatory pause.","Pause: next sinus impulse falls in refractory period.","Interval around ectopic ≈ 2 normal cycles.","Post-extrasystolic beat stronger (filling + Ca²⁺).","Sinus venosus stimulation – no compensatory pause.","VPC: full pause; APC: incomplete pause.","R-on-T can trigger VF.","Class III drugs prolong refractoriness."],"n":["Ventricular ARP ≈ 0.25–0.30 s","RRP ≈ 0.05 s","Pause ≈ 2 cycles"],"m":"**\"Skip and Squeeze\"** – the extrasystole is followed by a skipped beat (pause) and a strong squeeze.","v":["Why does a compensatory pause occur? The next sinus impulse reaches the ventricle while it is still refractory from the extrasystole.","Why is the post-extrasystolic beat stronger? Longer filling (Frank–Starling) and post-extrasystolic potentiation.","Why can't the heart be tetanised? Its absolute refractory period lasts through systole."]}},"ae|Stannius ligatures":{"d":"## Principle\n**Hermann Stannius (1852)** applied tight ligatures at the junctions of the frog heart's chambers to **block conduction** of the excitation wave. This demonstrates:\n1. The **origin of the heartbeat** in the **sinus venosus** (pacemaker).\n2. The **hierarchy of rhythmicity**: inherent rate **sinus venosus > atria > ventricle**.\n3. That impulses are **conducted** from sinus to atria to ventricle.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Pithed frog (or simulation); heart exposed and attached to Starling's lever. Record the **normal cardiogram** (rate).\n2. Pass a thread under the **aortae (truncus)** and around the heart.\n\n### First Stannius ligature\n- Tied at the **sinu-atrial junction** (along the white **crescentic line**).\n- **Result**:\n  - the **sinus venosus continues beating at its original rate**\n  - the **atria and ventricle stop** (temporarily) in **diastole**\n- After **≈ 15–30 min** (sources give minutes to an hour) the **atria and ventricle may resume** beating at a **slower, idioventricular-like rate**, as a new pacemaker emerges (**AV junction**).\n\n### Second Stannius ligature\n- Tied at the **atrioventricular junction** (groove), with the first ligature in place.\n- **Result**: the AV junctional tissue is **stimulated mechanically**, so the **ventricle starts beating** (at a slow rate). The atria remain quiescent, or beat independently if the ligature lies slightly on the atrial side.\n- The exact response depends on the ligature position:\n  - **on the ventricular side**: the ventricle beats\n  - **on the atrial side**: the atria may beat\n\n### Third Stannius ligature (apex)\n- Tied across the **lower third / apex of the ventricle**.\n- **Result**: the **apex does not beat spontaneously**, because it **lacks pacemaker tissue**, but it **responds to a stimulus** (excitable but not automatic).\n- An isolated apex is used for the **all-or-none** and **staircase** experiments.\n\n## Interpretation\n- **Pacemaker of the frog heart**: sinus venosus (≈ **mammalian SA node**).\n- The **sinus rate is the highest**, so it dominates (**overdrive suppression** of subsidiary pacemakers).\n- **Rates**: sinus venosus > atria > AV junction > ventricle. The apex is non-automatic.\n\n## Human correlates\n- **SA node**: **60–100/min** (intrinsic ≈ 100–110/min after autonomic blockade).\n- **AV node / junction**: **40–60/min**.\n- **Purkinje / ventricular**: **20–40/min**.\n- **Complete (3rd-degree) heart block** is the clinical counterpart of the first ligature with ventricular escape:\n  - atria at the sinus rate, **ventricles at an idioventricular rate (30–40)**\n  - **AV dissociation**, **cannon a waves**\n  - **Stokes–Adams attacks** during the pause before the escape rhythm starts (like the transient arrest after the first ligature)\n- **Artificial pacemakers** treat this.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Ligature **not tight** or misplaced (e.g. through the atria): partial conduction.\n- Damage to tissue from rough handling.\n- **Insufficient waiting time** after the first ligature: recovery missed.\n- Drying.\n- Not recording the rate before and after each step.\n- Temperature changes altering rate.","q":{"b":["Stannius 1852 – ligatures block conduction.","1st ligature: sinu-atrial junction (white crescentic line).","1st: sinus continues; atria + ventricle stop in diastole.","Later recovery at slower rate (new pacemaker).","2nd ligature: AV junction – ventricle starts beating (mechanical stimulation).","3rd: apex – no spontaneous beat but excitable.","Pacemaker = sinus venosus.","Hierarchy: sinus > atria > AV > ventricle.","Human: SA 60–100, AV 40–60, ventricle 20–40/min.","Clinical analogue: complete heart block; Stokes–Adams.","Overdrive suppression keeps subsidiary pacemakers silent."],"n":["SA 60–100/min","AV junction 40–60/min","Ventricle 20–40/min"],"m":"**\"1 Stops, 2 Starts, 3 Silent\"** – the three Stannius ligatures.","v":["Effect of the first Stannius ligature? The sinus venosus keeps beating; the atria and ventricle stop in diastole.","Why does the ventricle start beating after the second ligature? Mechanical stimulation of the AV junctional tissue.","What does the third (apex) ligature show? The apex is excitable but not automatic."]}},"ae|Effect of temperature on the frog heart":{"d":"## Principle\nThe frog is **poikilothermic**, and its heart **rate and force** depend directly on temperature.\n\n- **Pacemaker** (sinus venosus) activity depends on ion-channel kinetics, especially the **funny current (If)** and Ca²⁺ currents. These speed up with warmth (**Q₁₀ ≈ 2–3**).\n- Contraction and relaxation are enzymatic (myosin ATPase, SERCA), so they are faster in warmth.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Pithed frog heart on Starling's lever (or simulation); **slow drum**; time marker in seconds.\n2. Record a control at **room temperature** (≈ 20–25 °C), irrigating with Ringer at that temperature.\n3. Pour **cold Ringer (≈ 5–10 °C)** over the heart, **especially the sinus venosus**; record.\n4. Restore room temperature; record recovery.\n5. Pour **warm Ringer (≈ 30–35 °C; not above ≈ 37–40 °C)**; record.\n6. Restore. Optionally apply warm or cold Ringer **selectively to the ventricle only**: little change in rate. This shows that **rate is set by the pacemaker (sinus venosus)**.\n\n## Observations\n### Warm Ringer\n- **Heart rate increases** (**positive chronotropy**).\n- **Duration of each beat shortens**.\n- **Amplitude** may **decrease** (shorter filling time, shorter action potential, less Ca²⁺ entry).\n- Some texts note an initial increase in amplitude with moderate warmth. **AK Jain and most practical manuals: amplitude falls** at high rates.\n- **Very high temperature (≈ > 40 °C)**: **heat standstill / heat rigor**; irreversible protein coagulation.\n\n### Cold Ringer\n- **Heart rate decreases** (**negative chronotropy**).\n- **Duration of contraction and relaxation prolonged**.\n- **Amplitude increases** (longer filling, Starling effect; prolonged action potential and active state).\n- **Extreme cold**: heart stops (cold standstill), **reversible** on rewarming.\n\n### Selective application\n- Warming or cooling the **sinus venosus** alters the **rate**.\n- Warming or cooling the **ventricle** alters **only the force and shape** of its contraction, **not the rate**.\n- Proves the sinus venosus is the pacemaker.\n\n## Human relevance\n- **Fever**: heart rate rises **≈ 10 beats/min per °C** (≈ 18 per °C in some texts; a range is reported).\n- Exceptions: **relative bradycardia (Faget sign)** in **typhoid**, dengue, brucellosis, legionella. Typhoid is important in India.\n- **Hypothermia**:\n  - bradycardia\n  - **J (Osborn) waves** on ECG\n  - VF risk below ≈ 28 °C\n- Therapeutic hypothermia slows metabolism in cardiac surgery and after cardiac arrest (targeted temperature management).\n\n## Sources of error\n- Insufficient time for equilibration.\n- Ringer **too hot** (> 40 °C) causing irreversible damage.\n- Poor localisation of the application (sinus vs ventricle).\n- Drying between applications.\n- Fatigue or deterioration of the preparation.\n- Lever friction.\n- Not allowing return to baseline between tests.","q":{"b":["Frog is poikilothermic – heart rate follows temperature.","Warm: ↑ rate, shorter beats, amplitude usually ↓.","Cold: ↓ rate, longer beats, amplitude ↑.","Extreme heat: irreversible heat standstill (> ~40 °C).","Extreme cold: reversible standstill.","Warming sinus venosus changes rate; ventricle changes only force.","Proves sinus venosus is the pacemaker.","Pacemaker If and Ca²⁺ currents temperature-dependent (Q₁₀ 2–3).","Fever: HR ↑ ≈ 10/min per °C.","Relative bradycardia in typhoid (Faget sign).","Hypothermia: bradycardia, Osborn J waves."],"n":["Cold 5–10 °C","Warm 30–35 °C","Fever ≈ +10 bpm/°C","VF risk < 28 °C"],"m":"**\"Hot Heart Hurries, Cold Heart Crawls\"**.","v":["Effect of warm Ringer on the frog heart? Increased rate and usually decreased amplitude.","What does selective warming of the sinus venosus show? It is the pacemaker, because only warming it changes the rate.","Relative bradycardia in fever is seen in? Typhoid (Faget sign)."]}},"ae|Vagal stimulation and vagal escape":{"d":"## Principle\nIn the frog, **vagal (parasympathetic) and sympathetic fibres** run together in the **vagosympathetic trunk**.\n\n- Stimulating it releases **acetylcholine** at the heart.\n- ACh acts on **M₂ muscarinic receptors**, which:\n  - opens **K_ACh (GIRK) channels**, causing **hyperpolarisation**\n  - **reduces If and Ca²⁺ currents**, via Gi lowering cAMP\n\n**Effects**:\n- **negative chronotropic** (↓ rate)\n- **negative inotropic** (↓ force, mainly atrial in mammals)\n- **negative dromotropic** (↓ conduction)\n- reduced excitability (**bathmotropic**)\n\n**Otto Loewi (1921)** used vagal stimulation of the frog heart to discover chemical neurotransmission (\"**Vagusstoff**\" = ACh). Nobel Prize 1936.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Pithed frog (or simulation); heart on Starling's lever; **slow drum**.\n2. Locate the **vagosympathetic trunk**: it runs with the **petrohyoid muscles** and the laryngeal nerve, behind the angle of the jaw. Isolate it with a thread.\n3. Place it on electrodes. Stimulate with **faradic (repetitive) stimuli** of moderate strength for **≈ 15–30 s**, using a signal marker.\n4. Record during stimulation and after stopping.\n\n## Observations\n- **Latent period** before the effect: the first beat or two continue.\n- With **weak stimulation**: **bradycardia** and reduced amplitude.\n- With **strong stimulation**: **cardiac arrest in diastole**.\n- **Vagal escape**: despite **continued stimulation**, the heart **resumes beating** after some time (≈ 10–30 s), at first slowly and weakly.\n- **Post-stimulation**: on stopping, the heart beats **faster and stronger** for a while (**sympathetic after-effect**: the sympathetic fibres in the vagosympathetic trunk were also stimulated, and noradrenaline action lasts longer).\n\n## Mechanism of vagal escape (suggested; sources vary)\n- **Ventricular (idioventricular) escape**: subsidiary pacemakers in the AV junction or ventricle are less vagally inhibited and take over.\n- **Accumulation of sympathetic transmitter**: noradrenaline from the co-stimulated sympathetic fibres overcomes the vagal effect.\n- **Depletion or desensitisation of ACh** release or receptors (fatigue of the vagal endings).\n- **Hydrolysis of ACh by cholinesterase**.\n\n## Drug modification\n- **Atropine** (muscarinic blocker) **abolishes** the vagal effect. The sympathetic acceleration is then seen.\n- **Physostigmine / eserine** (anticholinesterase) **potentiates and prolongs** it.\n- **Nicotine** blocks the ganglia: preganglionic stimulation then becomes ineffective, but direct effects on the sinus still occur (classical **Langley** experiment).\n\n## Human relevance\n- **Vagal tone** keeps the resting human HR (~72) below the intrinsic SA rate (~100–110).\n- **Vasovagal syncope**.\n- **Carotid sinus massage / Valsalva** to terminate **SVT**.\n- **Athletes' bradycardia** (high vagal tone).\n- **Oculocardiac reflex** (traction on extraocular muscles in squint surgery). Treat with atropine.\n\n## Sources of error\n- Stimulus too weak (no effect) or too strong (current spread to the heart).\n- Nerve damaged or dried.\n- Electrodes touching other tissue.\n- Stimulation too long (fatigue).\n- Misidentifying the nerve.\n- Not marking the start and end of stimulation.","q":{"b":["Frog: vagal + sympathetic fibres in vagosympathetic trunk.","ACh → M₂ → ↑ K⁺ (GIRK), ↓ If, ↓ Ca²⁺.","Negative chrono-, ino-, dromo-, bathmotropic.","Strong stimulation → diastolic arrest.","Vagal escape: heart resumes despite continued stimulation.","Escape: subsidiary pacemakers, sympathetic NA, ACh depletion.","Post-stimulation acceleration – sympathetic after-effect.","Atropine abolishes; physostigmine potentiates.","Loewi 1921 – Vagusstoff (ACh).","Vagal tone keeps HR (~72) below intrinsic SA rate (~100–110).","Carotid sinus massage terminates SVT."],"n":["Stimulation 15–30 s","Escape ≈ 10–30 s","Resting HR ~72","Intrinsic SA ≈ 100–110"],"m":"Vagus does **\"4 Negatives\"** – chrono, ino, dromo, bathmo.","v":["What is vagal escape? Resumption of heartbeat despite continued vagal stimulation.","Who discovered chemical transmission using the frog heart? Otto Loewi (Vagusstoff = acetylcholine).","Effect of atropine on vagal stimulation? It abolishes the inhibitory effect."]}},"ae|Effects of drugs and ions on the frog heart":{"d":"## Principle\nCardiac excitability, rhythm and contractility depend on:\n- **ionic gradients** (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺)\n- **autonomic receptors** (β₁: Gs, cAMP; M₂: Gi)\n\nApplying **ions or drugs** to the frog heart (or simulation) demonstrates these dependencies. **Ringer (1882)** discovered the importance of **Ca²⁺** by accident: he used London tap water containing calcium.\n\n## Procedure\n1. Pithed frog heart on Starling's lever (or simulation); slow drum.\n2. Record a normal baseline.\n3. Apply **2–3 drops** of the test solution onto the heart. Record. Observe the onset and duration.\n4. **Wash with Ringer** until the baseline returns before the next drug.\n\n## Effects of ions\n- **Ca²⁺ excess** (e.g. 1–2% CaCl₂):\n  - **increased force** (positive inotropy)\n  - then **incomplete relaxation**, eventually **arrest in systole: \"calcium rigor\"**\n- **K⁺ excess** (e.g. 1–2% KCl):\n  - depolarises the resting membrane, **inactivates Na⁺ channels**\n  - **decreased rate and force**, then **arrest in diastole**\n  - basis of **cardioplegic solutions** in cardiac surgery\n  - clinical: **hyperkalaemia** causes tall T waves, bradyarrhythmias, asystole\n- **Na⁺ excess**: weakened contraction (less Ca²⁺ via the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger), with **diastolic arrest** in high concentrations.\n- **Ca²⁺ and K⁺ are antagonistic**: Ca²⁺ is used **clinically to protect the heart in hyperkalaemia** (calcium gluconate stabilises the membrane).\n\n## Effects of drugs\n- **Adrenaline / noradrenaline** (β₁):\n  - **↑ rate** (**positive chronotropy**)\n  - **↑ force** (**positive inotropy**)\n  - faster relaxation (**lusitropy**, via phospholamban phosphorylation)\n- **Acetylcholine** (M₂):\n  - **↓ rate and force**\n  - **diastolic arrest** at higher doses\n- **Atropine**: no effect alone on an isolated preparation (no vagal tone); **blocks ACh** given afterwards.\n- **Nicotine**: initial stimulation then **ganglionic block**.\n- **Digoxin** (cardiac glycosides):\n  - inhibits **Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase**, so intracellular Na⁺ rises, Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchange decreases, and **intracellular Ca²⁺ rises**\n  - **positive inotropy**\n  - toxic doses: **systolic arrest** (as with Ca²⁺) and arrhythmias\n  - **hypokalaemia potentiates digoxin toxicity**\n- **Physostigmine**: potentiates ACh.\n\n## Interpretation summary\n- **Systolic arrest**: Ca²⁺, digoxin (toxic).\n- **Diastolic arrest**: K⁺, ACh, high vagal stimulation.\n\n## Clinical relevance\n- **Electrolyte disturbances** cause arrhythmias:\n  - **hypokalaemia**: U waves, arrhythmias, digoxin toxicity\n  - **hypercalcaemia**: short QT\n  - **hypocalcaemia**: long QT\n- **Calcium gluconate** for hyperkalaemia.\n- **Potassium cardioplegia** in bypass surgery.\n- **Adrenaline in cardiac arrest** (1 mg IV every 3–5 min).\n- **Atropine for symptomatic bradycardia** (1 mg IV, repeat to a max of 3 mg; ACLS 2020).\n\n## Sources of error\n- Drug drops not reaching the pacemaker or ventricle.\n- **Inadequate washing** between drugs (carry-over).\n- Concentrations too high, causing irreversible damage.\n- Dried preparation.\n- Pacemaker damage.\n- Not recording the baseline.\n- Drug sequence confounding (e.g. ACh after atropine).","q":{"b":["Ringer discovered Ca²⁺ importance using tap water (1882).","Excess Ca²⁺: ↑ force → systolic arrest (calcium rigor).","Excess K⁺: ↓ rate/force → diastolic arrest (cardioplegia).","Ca²⁺ antagonises K⁺ – calcium gluconate in hyperkalaemia.","Adrenaline: +chrono, +ino, +lusitropy (β₁, cAMP).","ACh: −chrono, −ino → diastolic arrest (M₂).","Atropine blocks ACh.","Digoxin inhibits Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase → ↑ Ca²⁺ → +ino.","Hypokalaemia potentiates digoxin toxicity.","Systolic arrest: Ca²⁺, digoxin; diastolic: K⁺, ACh.","Wash with Ringer between drugs."],"n":["Drops 2–3","Adrenaline 1 mg q3–5 min (arrest)","Atropine 1 mg up to 3 mg"],"m":"**\"Calcium Contracts, Potassium Pauses\"** – Ca²⁺ gives systolic arrest; K⁺ gives diastolic arrest.","v":["What is calcium rigor? Arrest of the heart in systole from excess calcium.","Why is K⁺ used in cardioplegia? It depolarises the membrane, inactivates Na⁺ channels and arrests the heart in diastole.","Mechanism of digoxin's inotropic effect? Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase inhibition raises intracellular Ca²⁺ via the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger."]}},"ae|Perfusion of the isolated frog heart":{"d":"## Principle\nThe **isolated perfused heart** removes the effects of:\n- nerves (extrinsic autonomic control)\n- hormones\n- the circulation\n\nSo the **intrinsic properties** of the heart and the **direct effects of ions and drugs** can be studied under controlled conditions.\n\n- **Frog heart**: **Symes' cannula** (or Straub's) introduced into the ventricle via the **sinus venosus / inferior vena cava** (Symes) or the aorta. Ringer is perfused from a **Marriotte bottle** at constant pressure.\n- **Mammalian equivalent**: **Langendorff preparation (1895)**, retrograde perfusion via the **aorta** into the **coronary arteries**, the aortic valve closed by the pressure. Used for **rabbit, rat or guinea pig** hearts with oxygenated **Krebs–Henseleit** or **Locke's** solution at 37 °C.\n\n## Procedure (frog: traditional; simulated in CBME)\n1. Pithed frog; expose the heart.\n2. Ligate the vessels except the one to be cannulated; insert a **Symes cannula** filled with Ringer (no air bubbles) through the inferior vena cava and sinus into the ventricle.\n3. Tie it, then cut the heart free and mount it on a stand.\n4. Perfuse with Ringer from a reservoir at a **constant height / pressure**.\n5. Attach the apex to a **Starling's heart lever** (or a force transducer).\n6. Record a baseline; then:\n   - switch to test solutions (altered Ca²⁺, K⁺; drugs such as adrenaline, ACh, atropine, digoxin)\n   - or vary the **perfusion pressure / volume** (Starling's law)\n7. **Wash out** with plain Ringer between tests.\n\n## Results commonly demonstrated\n- **Ringer without Ca²⁺**: contractions weaken and stop (**excitation–contraction uncoupling**; electrical activity continues).\n- **Excess Ca²⁺**: **systolic arrest**.\n- **Excess K⁺**: **diastolic arrest**.\n- **Adrenaline**: ↑ rate and force. **ACh**: ↓ rate and force. **Atropine** blocks ACh.\n- **Increased filling (perfusion pressure)**: **↑ stroke volume / force** (**Frank–Starling law**, intrinsic heterometric regulation), up to a limit.\n- **Temperature effects** as in the intact heart.\n\n## Advantages\n- Precise control of composition, temperature and pressure.\n- Dose–response curves.\n- No neural or humoral interference.\n- Used historically to establish: **Loewi's** chemical transmission (**two-heart experiment**: perfusate from a vagally stimulated heart slowed a second heart), **Ringer's** Ca²⁺ discovery, and **Starling's** law in the **heart–lung preparation** (dog).\n\n## Mammalian (Langendorff) values\n- Perfusion pressure ≈ **60–80 mmHg**, or constant flow.\n- O₂ / CO₂ **95% / 5%**.\n- Coronary flow in a rat heart ≈ 10–15 ml/min.\n\n## Ethics in India\nUnder **CPCSEA** guidelines and **NMC/CBME**, UG teaching uses **computer simulations** (e.g. Langendorff or frog heart simulators) instead of live animals. Isolated organ work remains in **PG and research** settings with **IAEC approval**.\n\n## Sources of error\n- **Air bubbles in the cannula** (embolism, arrhythmia).\n- **Clot** blocking the cannula: avoid blood, or use heparin.\n- Damage to the **sinus venosus** during cannulation (loss of pacemaker).\n- Perfusion pressure fluctuating.\n- **Contamination** from previous drug solutions.\n- **Temperature** not controlled.\n- Leakage around the cannula.\n- Over-distension of the ventricle (beyond the Starling optimum).","q":{"b":["Isolated heart removes neural and humoral control.","Frog: Symes cannula via IVC / sinus venosus.","Mammal: Langendorff – retrograde aortic perfusion into coronaries.","Constant-pressure reservoir (Marriotte bottle).","Ca²⁺-free Ringer: contractions stop (E-C uncoupling).","Excess Ca²⁺ systolic arrest; excess K⁺ diastolic arrest.","Increased filling → stronger beat (Frank–Starling).","Loewi's two-heart experiment used perfused frog hearts.","Langendorff: Krebs–Henseleit, 95% O₂/5% CO₂, 37 °C.","CPCSEA/IAEC approval; UG uses simulations.","Avoid air bubbles and clots."],"n":["Langendorff 60–80 mmHg","95% O₂ / 5% CO₂","37 °C (mammal)"],"m":"**\"Langendorff Lands in Coronaries\"** – retrograde aortic perfusion.","v":["Why perfuse an isolated heart? To study its intrinsic properties and direct drug or ion effects without neural or humoral influences.","Route of perfusion in the Langendorff preparation? Retrograde through the aorta into the coronary arteries.","Effect of calcium-free Ringer? Contractions cease although electrical activity may continue."]}},"ae|Simulations and CAL in CBME (replacing live animals)":{"d":"## Background\nAnimal dissection and experimentation for **undergraduate teaching** were restricted in India by **MCI (2012) and UGC (2011) directives**:\n- **in line with** the **Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act (1960)** and **CPCSEA** guidelines\n- prompted by the protected status of **frogs** under the **Wildlife (Protection) Act** and declining amphibian populations\n\nUnder the **NMC Competency-Based Medical Education (CBME) curriculum (2019, revised 2023–24)**, the **amphibian and mammalian experiments** are taught through:\n- **computer-assisted learning (CAL)**\n- **simulations**\n- **videos**\n- **human experiments**\n\nLive animals are allowed only in research and PG settings, with **IAEC approval**.\n\n## Principle: the 3 Rs (Russell and Burch, 1959)\n- **Replacement**: use non-animal methods (simulations, cell cultures, human volunteers).\n- **Reduction**: fewer animals per result.\n- **Refinement**: minimise pain and distress.\n\nSome add a **4th R: Responsibility**.\n\n## Common tools\n- **Virtual physiology labs**: frog nerve–muscle and heart simulators (e.g. SimMuscle, SimHeart, **PhysioEx**, **Ex-Pharm**, AIIMS / Indian open-source simulators).\n- **Recorded kymograph videos** of the classical experiments.\n- **Data-acquisition systems (PowerLab, BIOPAC)** for **human** experiments:\n  - ECG\n  - EMG\n  - spirometry\n  - nerve conduction\n  - grip strength and fatigue\n  - reaction time\n- **Mannequins and simulators** for skills: CPR, IV cannulation, BP (Skills Lab mandated by NMC).\n- **Virtual reality / interactive 3D** anatomy-physiology apps.\n\n## Procedure (typical CAL session)\n1. **Briefing**: learning objectives (e.g. \"Demonstrate the effect of load on muscle contraction\", from the NMC competency list).\n2. Students **choose parameters** in the simulator (stimulus strength, frequency, load, temperature, drug dose).\n3. They **record virtual traces**, measure (latent period, amplitude, rate) and tabulate.\n4. They **plot graphs** (e.g. strength–response, length–tension, dose–response).\n5. **Interpretation and reflection**; viva; logbook entry (**certifiable competency**).\n\n## Advantages\n- **Ethical**; no animal suffering.\n- **Repeatable** and self-paced; can explore extreme conditions safely.\n- **No preparation failure** (drying, fatigue).\n- Cost-effective after set-up; available on phones and laptops.\n- **Standardised** results for assessment.\n\n## Limitations\n- No **hands-on dissection** or instrument-handling skills.\n- **Idealised data**: less appreciation of biological variability and experimental error.\n- Possible **passive learning** if not guided.\n- Needs infrastructure (computers, licences, internet).\n- **Evidence**: studies, including several Indian ones, show **comparable knowledge outcomes** to wet labs, though **student preference varies**.\n\n## Assessment (CBME)\n- **OSPE** stations with graphs and simulated data.\n- **DOAP** (Demonstrate, Observe, Assist, Perform) for human experiments.\n- Viva on principles.\n\n## Sources of error in simulations\n- Wrong **parameter entry** or unit confusion.\n- Misreading axes and scales.\n- Over-reliance on default settings.\n- Not repeating runs.\n- Copying outputs without interpretation.\n- Software version differences.","q":{"b":["UG animal experiments restricted in India (UGC 2011, MCI 2012).","Frogs protected under Wildlife (Protection) Act.","CBME (NMC 2019) uses CAL, simulations, videos.","3 Rs: Replacement, Reduction, Refinement (Russell & Burch 1959).","CPCSEA guidelines; IAEC approval for research.","Simulators: frog nerve–muscle, heart, Langendorff.","PowerLab/BIOPAC for human experiments.","Skills lab mannequins (CPR).","Advantages: ethical, repeatable, standardised.","Limitations: no hands-on skill, idealised data.","Assessment: OSPE, DOAP, logbook.","Knowledge outcomes comparable to wet lab."],"n":["3 Rs (1959)","CBME 2019","PCA Act 1960","MCI 2012 / UGC 2011"],"m":"**3 Rs** – **Replace, Reduce, Refine** (+ Responsibility).","v":["What are the 3 Rs? Replacement, Reduction and Refinement of animal use.","Which body regulates animal experimentation in India? CPCSEA, through institutional animal ethics committees (IAEC).","One limitation of simulations? No hands-on skill, and idealised data hide biological variability."]}}});